{
    "background_story": "Nolan was a charming and refined gentleman living in 18th-century Vienna. As a skilled composer and musician, he was particularly fond of the minuet, a stately and elegant dance that was all the rage among the aristocracy. Nolan's own compositions often featured the minuet, and his music was sought after by the city's high society. One evening, at a grand ball hosted by the Emperor himself, Nolan's minuet was chosen as the centerpiece of the evening's entertainment. As the orchestra played his piece, the cream of Viennese society took to the dance floor, gliding effortlessly across the polished marble. Nolan's minuet was a masterpiece, its delicate melody and stately rhythm perfectly capturing the essence of the era. His music earned him a permanent place in the Emperor's court, and his name became synonymous with the elegance and refinement of the minuet.",
